{
  "ir_hash": "sha256:207656d3e29f4b93c736ad73d37f5093852a853fc2b68540f12d19e383f2d91b",
  "knowledges": [
    {
      "content": "TPO decouples RL-based agent optimization into two independent steps: (1) build a target distribution q from environment feedback (solving Q1: what should the agent do?), (2) cross-entropy-fit the policy to q (solving Q2: how to update the agent?). This eliminates the critic, value function, and off-policy correction that make PPO/GRPO/DG fragile.",
      "content_hash": "7a5535d923a7978fe33071b70dbc29848a853557f40f6d7f1c57c6699c381539",
      "declaration_index": 0,
      "exported": false,
      "id": "github:2604_06159::tpo_decouples_rl",
      "label": "tpo_decouples_rl",
      "metadata": {
        "aggregated_from": [
          "claim_tpo_decouples",
          "claim_pg_entangles_two_questions",
          "claim_entanglement_makes_pg_fragile"
        ],
        "prior": 0.9,
        "prior_justification": "Closed-form target distribution + supervised cross-entropy fit; mathematically precise. -0.05: η is a hyperparameter; -0.05: scalar feedback assumption."
      },
      "module": "motivation",
      "parameters": [],
      "title": "TPO decouples Q1 (target) from Q2 (update) — no critic needed",
      "type": "claim"
    },
    {
      "content": "PG/PPO/GRPO/DG fail to learn on sparse-reward tasks because the policy gradient vanishes when most trajectories earn zero reward. TPO's decoupled design avoids this: the target distribution is built from all available feedback (including zeros), and the cross-entropy fit provides a dense learning signal regardless of reward sparsity. On token reversal (sparse reward), TPO solves the task while GRPO/DG fail to leave random baseline. This is a 'solve vs not-learn' gap, not a small delta.",
      "content_hash": "69a0c65d52492830996447a86cbe23631c4da2217e3e8294cb4c64c20c8c0bf4",
      "declaration_index": 1,
      "exported": false,
      "id": "github:2604_06159::tpo_excels_on_sparse_reward",
      "label": "tpo_excels_on_sparse_reward",
      "metadata": {
        "aggregated_from": [
          "claim_fig1_sparse_outperform",
          "claim_fig1_sparse_qualitative_gap",
          "claim_fig1_dense_match",
          "claim_entanglement_makes_pg_fragile"
        ],
        "prior": 0.85,
        "prior_justification": "Token reversal qualitative 'solve vs not-learn' gap; replicated on neural bandit and sequence tasks. -0.08: PG baselines may be undertuned; -0.07: single-step only."
      },
      "module": "motivation",
      "parameters": [],
      "title": "TPO excels where PG methods fail: sparse-reward tasks",
      "type": "claim"
    },
    {
      "content": "On dense-reward tasks (MNIST bandit, WebArena-Turbo), TPO matches or slightly exceeds PG-based methods (PPO, GRPO, DG). It achieves 59.2% on WebArena-Turbo (+16.2pp over prompt engineering SPA's 43%). The key insight is that TPO's design loses nothing on easy tasks while gaining decisively on hard ones.",
      "content_hash": "d7796932fbc78f17e68c7a1728290f0f78b01eff79d429ac5e39e906e5aedd29",
      "declaration_index": 2,
      "exported": false,
      "id": "github:2604_06159::tpo_matches_on_dense_reward",
      "label": "tpo_matches_on_dense_reward",
      "metadata": {
        "aggregated_from": [
          "claim_fig1_dense_match",
          "claim_tpo_webarena_result",
          "claim_headline_contribution"
        ],
        "prior": 0.82,
        "prior_justification": "MNIST bandit + WebArena-Turbo show TPO ≈ PG on dense tasks. -0.10: WebArena-Turbo is a simplified split; -0.08: 7B scale only."
      },
      "module": "motivation",
      "parameters": [],
      "title": "TPO matches PG methods on dense reward, dominates on sparse",
      "type": "claim"
    },
    {
      "content": "TPO requires no critic network, no value function, no GAE, no off-policy correction, and no KL penalty tuning. The core loop is: sample trajectories → score with environment → build target q → cross-entropy step. This simplicity eliminates multiple hyperparameters and failure modes that make PG-based agent training brittle in practice.",
      "content_hash": "1256f85568b76f7e5de058563fb0d66d2110d54c3d13b93911e5879b1cf871c4",
      "declaration_index": 3,
      "exported": false,
      "id": "github:2604_06159::tpo_simplicity_advantage",
      "label": "tpo_simplicity_advantage",
      "metadata": {
        "aggregated_from": [
          "claim_tpo_decouples",
          "claim_pg_entangles_two_questions"
        ],
        "prior": 0.88,
        "prior_justification": "No critic / value / GAE / off-policy correction follows from the decoupled formulation — an engineering fact, not an empirical claim."
      },
      "module": "motivation",
      "parameters": [],
      "title": "TPO eliminates critic, GAE, KL penalty — simpler and more robust",
      "type": "claim"
    },
    {
      "content": "TPO experiments span tabular bandits, neural bandits, sequence tasks, and WebArena-Turbo. However, it has not been tested on multi-turn, multi-step agent tasks (e.g., SWE-bench, multi-hop web navigation) where the action space is compositional and the credit assignment problem is harder.",
      "content_hash": "e0b3e6fa846df2f9c0f520d1941415f3f092f943d71dc3e18cc71ef273f93595",
      "declaration_index": 4,
      "exported": false,
      "id": "github:2604_06159::bandit_and_single_task",
      "label": "bandit_and_single_task",
      "metadata": {
        "prior": 0.93,
        "prior_justification": "Documented experimental scope; factual."
      },
      "module": "motivation",
      "parameters": [],
      "title": "Limitation: single-step or simple multi-step tasks only",
      "type": "claim"
    },
    {
      "content": "TPO compares against PPO, GRPO, DG, and prompt engineering baselines but not against RLHF/DPO-based methods that use preference data. This is appropriate for the paper's scope (online RL for agents) but limits claims about TPO vs the broader RL optimization landscape.",
      "content_hash": "cc72987f1d46157909e298eedc59755f0726596d3524ffd45f7d49230fe96810",
      "declaration_index": 5,
      "exported": false,
      "id": "github:2604_06159::no_rlhf_comparison",
      "label": "no_rlhf_comparison",
      "metadata": {
        "prior": 0.95,
        "prior_justification": "Paper's explicit scoping choice."
      },
      "module": "motivation",
      "parameters": [],
      "title": "Limitation: no comparison with preference-based methods",
      "type": "claim"
    },
    {
      "content": "PG baselines used default hyperparameters; a dedicated PG tuning study on sparse-reward tasks has not been done. Critic-based methods with better exploration (RND, curiosity) might close the gap.",
      "content_hash": "bffda66c71f2d9bdfa1478b1d7921a38e23196d73efb3643e0fa27c9a7e138ba",
      "declaration_index": 0,
      "exported": false,
